course: medicine
lesson: 41
title: Football: positions, rules and scoring
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Football looks simple, kick the ball into the goal, but the Laws of the Game keep it fair and safe: only the goalkeeper may handle the ball, and only in their own penalty area.
explain: Juniors in England start small, 7 against 7, then 9 against 9 from Under-11, on bigger pitches as they grow, so every child gets lots of touches of the ball.
explain: Picture a striker standing next to the keeper all game waiting for a long ball: the linesman's flag goes up every time, because that is offside. Stay level with the second-last defender, then time your run.
explain: A goal counts only when the whole ball crosses the whole line. Shin pads are compulsory, studs-up tackles are fouls, and young players don't practise heading, because their brains need protecting.
fact: The goalkeeper is the only player allowed to handle the ball, and only inside their own penalty area.
fact: In England juniors play 7v7 at Under-9 and Under-10 and 9v9 at Under-11 and Under-12; small-sided games give every child more touches.
fact: Simply put, you are offside if, when a teammate passes forward, you are in the other team's half and nearer their goal line than both the ball and the second-last defender; being level is onside.
fact: Just standing in an offside position is not an offence; getting involved in play is.
fact: There is no offside straight from a corner kick, a throw-in or a goal kick.
fact: A goal counts only when the whole ball has crossed the whole goal line between the posts and under the bar.
fact: In a league a win is worth 3 points and a draw 1.
fact: In a penalty shootout each side takes 5 kicks, then one each if it's still level; the keeper must keep part of one foot on or in line with the goal line until the ball is kicked.
fact: A reckless tackle, like sliding in studs up from behind, is a foul even if you touch the ball first; two yellow cards in one game make a red, and the player must leave.
fact: FA guidance in England says primary-age children don't practise heading in training, and it was reported that deliberate heading is being taken out of matches for under-12s.
fact: Professional midfielders often cover about 10 to 12 km in a match, in a mix of walking, jogging and sprinting.
fact: Shin pads are required by the Laws of the Game; the front of the shin bone has no muscle to cushion a kick.
fact: A fair throw-in uses both hands, from behind and over the head, with part of both feet on the ground on or behind the touchline; a foul throw gives the throw to the other team.
exercise: Oliver from Leeds is put in goal. What can a keeper do that others can't? | Handle the ball inside their own penalty area | Handle the ball anywhere on the pitch | Score by throwing the ball with their hands | Leave the pitch whenever they like to fetch the ball
exercise: In a 7v7 game, how many players are on the pitch? | 14 | 7 | 22 | 11
exercise: Aisha moves up to Under-11. What format do Under-11s play in England? | 9v9 | 11v11 | 5v5 | 7v7
exercise: Why do young children play small-sided games? | Every child gets far more touches of the ball | Small pitches stop all injuries in matches | Children can't keep track of eleven players at once | Coaches don't own any big pitches
exercise: Jayden stands by the keeper all match waiting for long passes. What's the call? | Offside, when he gets involved in play | A goal every time he scores from there | A penalty to his own team | Nothing, goal-hanging is always allowed
exercise: A striker is exactly level with the second-last defender when the pass is played. Offside? | No, level is onside | Yes, being level counts as offside | Only if it's a long pass over the top of the defence | Only in the second half of the game
exercise: Siobhan heads in straight from a corner from behind the defence. Offside? | No, there's no offside from a corner kick | Yes, she was behind the defenders when it came over | Yes, headers from corners are always offside | Only if the keeper touched it on the way in
exercise: Liam's shot drops onto the goal line, partly over. Goal? | No, the whole ball must cross the whole line | Yes, touching the line is enough for a goal to count | Yes, if it hit the post first | Only if the crowd cheers loudly enough
exercise: A team wins 2 and draws 1. How many league points? | 7 | 3 | 5 | 9
exercise: Priya's penalty in a shootout. What must the keeper do? | Keep a foot on or in line with the line until the kick | Run out to meet the ball early, before the kick | Stand on the penalty spot next to the taker | Turn their back on the taker until the kick
exercise: How many kicks does each side take in a shootout before sudden death? | 5 | 3 | 11 | 1
exercise: Kai slides in studs up from behind but touches the ball first. Foul? | Yes, reckless tackles are fouls | No, touching the ball first is always fair | Only if the ref saw blood on the ankle | Only in professional matches on TV
exercise: A player gets two yellow cards. What happens? | They get a red and leave the pitch | They have to take a penalty for the other team | They sit out five minutes in the sin bin | Nothing happens, only red cards count
exercise: Ellie from Newcastle wants the team to practise headers for ages. What does FA guidance say for primary-age kids? | No heading practice in training | Practise headers every single training session | Head only with a heavy leather ball | Heading is fine as long as you wear a hat
exercise: Mohammed thinks a pro midfielder "just jogs". About how far does one often cover in a match? | About 10 to 12 km | About 500 m | About 50 km | About 1 km
exercise: Noah turns up with no shin pads. What happens? | The ref doesn't let him play | He plays, but only in goal, where it's safer | Nothing, pads are optional at his age | He plays the first half only, then goes off
exercise: Zara takes a throw-in with one hand, one foot in the air. Result? | A foul throw: the other team takes it | A free kick to her own team for a good throw | A goal kick to her own team | Nothing, it's a fair throw
test: Oliver: "Goalie? That's proper mardy, I wanna score!" What's true about keepers? | They often save the match, and every position teaches something | They're always the weakest player in the team | They're not allowed to move off their line | They can never score a goal
test: "Pitch is massive, it's giving desert." Aisha's first 9v9. Why does she get so tired? | More space means more running, so heart and lungs work harder | Big pitches have thinner air across them | 9v9 games last six hours with no break | The ball is twice as heavy
test: The other coach shouts, "Ref, you're blind!" when Siobhan scores from a corner. Who's right? | The ref: there's no offside from a corner | The coach: she was behind the defence when it was kicked | Neither: corners can't be headed in at all | The coach: headers from corners never count
test: "It's in, no cap, it touched the line!" What would the ref give? | No goal, unless the whole ball crossed the line | A goal, because the line is part of the goal and it touched | A penalty, because it hit the post first | A replay of the shot from the same place
test: Mate: "Don't bottle it, Priya!" Her legs are jelly. What helps? | A few slow breaths out and pick a corner early | Changing her mind about the corner at the very last second | Shutting her eyes tight as she kicks it | Running at full speed from far away
test: Kai: "Shut it, I got the ball first!" Studs on the striker's ankle. Who's right? | The ref: how you tackle matters, it's a foul | Kai: getting the ball first is always fair, whatever the studs did | Nobody: tackles from behind can't be fouls | Kai, but only if he says sorry
test: What would happen to a 9v9 team if one player got a red card? | They'd play with 8 | They'd play with 10 | They'd lose the match at once | Nothing, they'd just bring on a sub
test: "Headers are harmless with a light ball, man." What is the guidance trying to avoid? | Repeated knocks to a young brain | Footballs getting dirty and worn | Players growing too tall too quickly | Coaches getting bored in training
test: Mohammed: "That midfielder's just jogging, proper mid." What would you see on the stats? | Often 10 km or more in walks, jogs and sprints | Less than half a kilometre in the whole game, mostly walking | Only standing still and passing | A marathon every match
test: Noah: "Pads are for wimps, I'll be grand." What's true? | Every player, even at the World Cup, must wear them | Only little kids wear pads, pros don't | Pads slow you down so pros skip them | Pads are only for the keepers
test: Zara: "Ag, that was a lekker (great) throw, hey!" One hand, foot up. What's the fair way? | Both hands, from behind the head, both feet down | One hand, if that makes the ball go further | A run-up and a jump from behind the line | Kicking it back in from the line
test: In a cartoon, a goalie catches the ball in the other team's box. Real rule? | Keepers may only handle in their own penalty area | Keepers may handle anywhere on the pitch, in any box | Keepers may never use their hands at all | Only defenders may handle it in the box
exam: Identify the sole player permitted to handle the ball, and the area within which such handling is lawful. | The goalkeeper, within their own penalty area | Any defender, within their own half of the field of play | The captain, anywhere on the field of play | The goalkeeper, anywhere on the field
exam: Select the response consistent with the Laws of the Game concerning an attacker level with the second-last opponent when the ball is played. | The attacker is in an onside position | The attacker is in an offside position | The attacker must be cautioned by the referee | The goal must be disallowed
exam: Pursuant to the Laws of the Game, ascertain the condition under which a goal is awarded. | The whole of the ball has passed over the whole of the goal line | The ball has made contact with the goal line | The ball has struck the goalpost | Any part of the ball has crossed the line
exam: It is given that a team records two victories and one draw, a victory earning three points and a draw one. Determine the aggregate number of points accrued. | Seven | Three | Five | Nine
